Done. All tests pass. Here's what I implemented:

## Changes

**`knapsack.h`** — added the function declaration (and `<vector>` include):
```cpp
int maximum_value(int max_weight, const std::vector<Item>& items);
```

**`knapsack.cpp`** — implemented the classic 0/1 knapsack dynamic program:
```cpp
int maximum_value(int max_weight, const std::vector<Item>& items)
{
    if (max_weight < 0) return 0;

    // best[w] = maximum value achievable with a knapsack of capacity w
    std::vector<int> best(max_weight + 1, 0);

    for (const auto& item : items)
    {
        // Iterate capacity in descending order so each item is used at most once
        for (int capacity = max_weight; capacity >= item.weight; --capacity)
        {
            best[capacity] = std::max(best[capacity],
                                      best[capacity - item.weight] + item.value);
        }
    }

    return best[max_weight];
}
```

## How it works

`best[w]` tracks the maximum value achievable with capacity `w`. For each item, the capacity loop runs **descending** (`max_weight` down to `item.weight`), which guarantees each item is considered at most once (0/1 semantics) — a single item's contribution can't be reused within the same pass. Items heavier than the knapsack are simply skipped since the inner loop never executes for them.

## Verification

- All **7 test cases pass** (7 assertions), including the six gated behind `EXERCISM_RUN_ALL_TESTS`: no items, one too-heavy item, the two anti-greedy cases (by weight and by value), the example knapsack (90), 8 items (900), and 15 items (1458).
- The solution compiles cleanly under the same strict flags the CMake build uses (`-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror`).
- No new files were added and the tests/build files are untouched; all temporary build artifacts were removed.
